/*!
    \page android-security.html
    \title Android Security Considerations
    \brief Security considerations when writing Qt applications for Android.
    \ingroup security

    Other applications on the device reach a Qt for Android application through
    intents, activity results, and the clipboard. Qt passes the data from these
    channels to your application unchanged, but it does not check whether the
    data is safe to use. This page describes what each channel exposes and what
    your application must check.

    Qt for Android treats the Android system as trusted and every other
    application as untrusted, so everything arriving on these channels is
    \l{Handling Untrusted Data}{untrusted data}. For how this fits into the
    wider division of responsibility, see the \l{Qt Shared Security Model}. For
    the tools that build your application, see
    \l{Using Qt's Developer Tools Securely}.

    \section1 Incoming intents

    Qt handles intents whose action is \c ACTION_VIEW. It takes the intent's
    data URI and passes it to QDesktopServices::openUrl(), which dispatches to
    the handler you registered with QDesktopServices::setUrlHandler(). Qt does
    not validate that URI, because only your application knows which schemes,
    hosts, and paths are meaningful to it.

    If you declare a matching intent filter, any application on the device can
    start your activity with any URI it chooses. A web page can also start your
    activity through a link. Validate the scheme, the host, and the path before
    you use them. Never treat a path from a URI as a trusted file system
    location.

    \section1 Activity results

    When you start another activity for a result, Qt passes the returned intent
    to your result callback unchanged. Qt does not inspect it. The activity that
    produced the result might not be the one you expected, because any
    application can register for the same action.

    Check the request code, check the result code, and validate the intent's
    contents before using them.

    \section1 Clipboard content

    QClipboard on Android reads the system clipboard, which any application can
    write. A QMimeData obtained from it can carry any format and any payload
    those applications chose to put there.

    Treat clipboard content like a downloaded file. Validate it before you
    render it as markup, pass it to a shell, or build a file path from it.

    \section1 Recommendations

    \list
        \li \b{Validate at the entry point.} Validate data in the URL handler,
            in the activity result receiver, and where you read the clipboard.
            Deeper in the application, the origin of the data is no longer
            clear.
        \li \b{Declare only the intent filters you need.} Every filter widens
            the set of applications that can reach you, and an exported
            activity is reachable by any application on the device.
        \li \b{Prefer allowlists.} Accept the schemes, hosts, and MIME types
            you support and reject everything else. Do not try to list all
            dangerous values.
        \li \b{Do not infer trust from the channel.} A URI from an intent does
            not show who sent it.
    \endlist
*/
